Toward a Decision‑Centric Theory of Contact Intelligence

Author: Ouadi Maakoul

Abstract

Contact intelligence—the capacity of a robot to purposefully exploit physical interactions with its environment—requires a unified treatment of geometry, uncertainty, information, safety, and computation. This paper develops the Future Contact Advantage (FCA) framework, a decision‑theoretic foundation that quantifies the long‑term value of a contact primitive as a single scalar advantage function. We model the robot as a hybrid stochastic system evolving on a compact admissible configuration manifold; body velocities are decomposed via a rigorous Hodge–Helmholtz splitting that separates locomotion from shape change. The belief‑space partially observable Markov decision process is endowed with a reward that trades off task progress against information gain, energy expenditure, sensor usage, and computational cost. We prove a geometric lower bound showing that contacts with large locomotion components are intrinsically valuable; a Wasserstein‑robustness bound guaranteeing performance under distributional ambiguity; an information‑theoretic bound that links expected value improvement to the square root of mutual information; and submodularity of sensor selection, justifying a greedy activation policy. Safety is enforced through hierarchical control barrier functions with an exact penalty. The main result provides an integrated performance guarantee for a greedy policy that maximizes the FCA, accounting for horizon truncation, value‑function approximation error, and solver suboptimality. The framework is instantiated in a three‑layer computational architecture—offline macro‑action generation, online reduced‑order complementarity‑free model predictive control, and a reactive CBF‑CLF quadratic program—whose per‑decision complexity is explicitly bounded. The FCA provides a normative, resource‑aware calculus for contact intelligence, offering rigorous guarantees that are largely independent of the particular learning implementation and setting a mathematical foundation for physically interactive humanoid robots.

---

1. Introduction

Contact is the defining characteristic of physical intelligence. Whether a humanoid climbs a ladder, a quadruped wedges a foot into a crevice, or a manipulator slides a peg into a hole, the ability to deliberately make and break contacts distinguishes embodied agents from purely kinematic motion planners. Yet contact also introduces the most challenging aspects of robotics: hybrid dynamics with discontinuous jumps, non‑Gaussian and multiplicative uncertainty, abrupt changes in the geometry of feasible motion, and the constant danger of catastrophic failure. A robot in the field must simultaneously answer four questions for every contact decision: Can I move in the direction I need? (geometry), What might go wrong if my model is off? (robustness), How much will I learn about the world? (information), and Is this safe and affordable? (resources). The robotics literature has treated these questions largely in isolation: geometric mechanics studies reachable body velocities, stochastic control provides robustness certificates, information theory quantifies sensing value, and control barrier functions enforce safety. No principled, unified decision theory for contact has existed, leading to brittle, hand‑crafted contact schedules or, at the other extreme, purely data‑driven policies that lack guarantees.

This paper introduces the Future Contact Advantage (FCA) framework, a decision‑centric theory that synthesizes the four questions into a scalar advantage function—the amount by which a candidate contact improves (or worsens) the robot’s long‑term prospects, net of all costs. The central object is a partially observable Markov decision process (POMDP) defined over a hybrid belief state; the reward function explicitly encodes task progress, an information bonus weighted by belief entropy, and penalties for energy, sensing, and computation. The optimal value function V^* satisfies the Bellman equation, and the Future Contact Advantage of a contact primitive c at belief b is defined as

\operatorname{FCA}(b,c) = \max_{a\in\mathcal{A}_c(b)} Q^*(b,c,a) - V^*(b),

where Q^* is the state‑action value function. By construction, \max_c \operatorname{FCA}(b,c)=0 and the optimal contact attains zero; FCA thus measures how much a contact deviates from optimality. Maximizing FCA greedily yields a principled contact selection policy, and the bulk of the paper is devoted to proving that this greedy policy is near‑optimal, with every source of suboptimality explicitly bounded.

The mathematical backbone rests on differential geometry, stochastic processes, information theory, optimal control, and trajectory optimization. After establishing rigorous definitions for the hybrid state, the contact manifold, and the belief dynamics, the paper proceeds through a sequence of results that isolate the fundamental components of FCA:

· Geometry (Theorem 1): A contact’s value is lower‑bounded by the projection of its locomotion subspace onto a task direction, independently of any value‑function approximation.
· Robustness (Theorem 2): Under distributional ambiguity modeled by a Wasserstein ball around the nominal belief transition, the worst‑case expected value decreases by at most a term proportional to the ambiguity radius and the Lipschitz constant of V^*.
· Information (Theorems 3 & 4): The expected value gain from observations is bounded below by a constant times the square root of mutual information, and mutual information is submodular in the set of activated sensors, justifying a greedy sensor activation policy.
· Safety (Theorem 5): An exact penalty formulation using hierarchical control barrier functions enforces safety without introducing infinite values.
· Performance Guarantee (Theorem 6): The greedy FCA policy, implemented with an approximate value network, a complementarity‑free contact solver, and a finite lookahead, achieves a value within an additive gap of the optimal, where the gap decomposes into terms from horizon truncation, value‑function approximation, and solver suboptimality.

The final part of the paper describes a three‑timescale computational architecture that realizes FCA on current humanoid hardware, and we outline a validation roadmap. The core contribution is a normative standard for contact intelligence: FCA is not a hand‑crafted heuristic or a black‑box learned policy, but an advantage function derived from first principles of physics, information, and computation, accompanied by rigorous bounds. This provides both a practical algorithm for contact selection and a theoretical benchmark against which any contact decision‑maker can be evaluated.

