3. Future Contact Advantage

The planning problem is a constrained POMDP. Over beliefs it reduces to a fully observable MDP with composite actions u=(c,a). The instantaneous reward includes task reward, an information bonus, and resource penalties:

\begin{aligned}
r(b,c,a) = &\; R_{\text{task}}(b,a) + \beta(b)\, I(X'; Z \mid b,c) \\
& - \lambda_E \Delta E(c) - \lambda_S \Delta\mathcal{S}(c) - \lambda_{\mathcal{K}} \Delta\mathcal{K}(c),
\end{aligned}

where \beta(b) increases with belief entropy, encouraging exploration when uncertainty is high, and \Delta E, \Delta\mathcal{S}, \Delta\mathcal{K} are energy, sensing, and computation costs, respectively. The optimal value function V^* satisfies the Bellman equation

V^*(b) = \max_{c\in\mathcal{C}}\max_{a\in\mathcal{A}_c(b)} \big\{ r(b,c,a) + \gamma\,\mathbb{E}_{z}[ V^*(\tau(b,c,a,z)) ] \big\}. \tag{1}

Definition (Future Contact Advantage). The FCA of a primitive c is the contact‑level advantage function

\boxed{\operatorname{FCA}(b, c) = \max_{a \in \mathcal{A}_c(b)} Q^*(b, c, a) \;-\; V^*(b)}, \tag{2}

where Q^*(b,c,a) = r(b,c,a) + \gamma\,\mathbb{E}_z[ V^*(\tau(b,c,a,z)) ]. By construction, \max_c \operatorname{FCA}(b,c) = 0 and the optimal contact c^*(b) attains zero. FCA quantifies the value of committing to contact c relative to the best possible contact, with actions optimized accordingly. It is an advantage over contact primitives, distinct from the standard state‑action advantage A(b,a)=Q(b,a)-V(b).

