4. Geometric Value of Contacts

We assume the task reward is strictly increasing in the projection of the expected body velocity onto a fixed direction \mathbf{d}\in\mathfrak{se}(3). Let \mathcal{V}_{\text{adm}} be the set of body velocities achievable under torque and stability constraints over the compact admissible configuration manifold.

Theorem 1 (Geometric Lower Bound).
For any contact c,

\max_{a\in\mathcal{A}_c(b)} R_{\text{task}}(b,a) \;\ge\; R_{\text{task,max}}\!\left( \max_{\mathbf{v}\in V_c \cap \mathcal{V}_{\text{adm}}} \langle \mathbf{v}_{\text{loc}}, \mathbf{d}\rangle \right).

Proof sketch. The robot’s dynamics are fully actuated in the joint space. On the compact manifold \mathcal{Q}_{\text{adm}}, the system is small‑time locally controllable; thus any velocity in \mathcal{V}_{\text{adm}}\cap V_c can be approached arbitrarily closely by a sequence of controls over a short time horizon. The monotonicity and continuity of the reward yield the bound. The Hodge–Helmholtz decomposition is orthogonal in the kinetic energy metric, so \langle\mathbf{v},\mathbf{d}\rangle = \langle\mathbf{v}_{\text{loc}},\mathbf{d}\rangle + \langle\mathbf{v}_{\text{curl}},\mathbf{d}\rangle. Restricting to \mathbf{v}_{\text{curl}}=0 gives the lower bound in terms of the locomotion component alone. ∎

This theorem provides a purely geometric criterion: contacts whose contact maps span large locomotion components in task‑relevant directions are inherently valuable, irrespective of any value function approximation.

---

5. Robustness to Distributional Uncertainty

Real contact interactions introduce non‑Gaussian, multiplicative uncertainty. We capture this with a Wasserstein ambiguity set.

Definition. For a nominal belief transition \hat p(\cdot\mid b,c,a), the robust transition set is

\mathfrak{P}(b,c,a) = \{\, p \in \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{B}) \mid W_1(p, \hat p) \le \epsilon \,\},

with radius \epsilon>0, where W_1 is the 1‑Wasserstein distance on belief space with a suitable base metric. The robust FCA is

\operatorname{FCA}_{\text{rob}}(b,c) = \max_{a} \inf_{p\in\mathfrak{P}(b,c,a)} \big( r(b,c,a) + \gamma\, \mathbb{E}_{b'\sim p}[V^*(b')] \big) - V^*(b).

Theorem 2 (Robust Lower Bound).
Assume V^* is L-Lipschitz with respect to W_1 on the belief space. Then

\operatorname{FCA}_{\text{rob}}(b,c) \;\ge\; \operatorname{FCA}_{\text{nom}}(b,c) \;-\; \gamma L \epsilon,

where \operatorname{FCA}_{\text{nom}} is computed with the nominal transition \hat p.

Proof. By the Kantorovich–Rubinstein duality, \inf_{p: W_1\le\epsilon} \mathbb{E}_p[V^*] \ge \mathbb{E}_{\hat p}[V^*] - L\epsilon. Substituting into the definition of FCA yields the inequality. ∎

Thus, even under severe model misspecification, the conservative FCA penalizes each contact by an amount proportional to the ambiguity radius and the Lipschitz constant of the value function.

