6. Information Value and Sensor Selection

Observations under contact c reduce both environmental uncertainty (model \mathcal{M}) and self‑state uncertainty. We derive a general lower bound on the expected value improvement using the mutual information and the Lipschitz property of V^*.

Theorem 3 (Value of Information Lower Bound).
If V^* is L-Lipschitz with respect to the total variation distance on beliefs, then for any contact c,

\mathbb{E}[V^*(b'_c)] - \mathbb{E}[V^*(b'_0)] \;\ge\; \frac{L}{\sqrt{2}} \sqrt{\,I(X'; Z \mid b,c)}.

Proof. Pinsker’s inequality gives \mathrm{TV}(p,q) \le \sqrt{\frac12 D_{\text{KL}}(p\|q)}. By the Lipschitz property, \mathbb{E}_p[V^*] - \mathbb{E}_q[V^*] \ge -L\,\mathrm{TV}(p,q). The mutual information is the expectation of the KL divergence between the posterior and the prior. Using these facts and Jensen’s inequality yields the square‑root bound. ∎

This bound, while weaker than a linear one, avoids the degenerate zero‑constant problem and is universally valid under a mild Lipschitz assumption. The information bonus in the reward function can thus be set proportional to \sqrt{I(X';Z)} to align with the guaranteed value improvement.

Sensor subset selection. The sensing cost \Delta\mathcal{S}(c) can be reduced by activating only a minimal set of sensors. The effective information contribution becomes I(X'; Z_\mathcal{Z} \mid b,c). The function f(\mathcal{Z}) = I(X'; Z_\mathcal{Z} \mid b,c) is monotone and submodular (Theorem 4). The proof is immediate: for any \mathcal{A}\subseteq\mathcal{B} and s\notin\mathcal{B},

f(\mathcal{A}\cup\{s\}) - f(\mathcal{A}) = I(X'; Z_s \mid Z_\mathcal{A}) \ge I(X'; Z_s \mid Z_\mathcal{B}) = f(\mathcal{B}\cup\{s\}) - f(\mathcal{B}),

since conditioning reduces entropy. Consequently, a greedy sensor activation policy achieves a (1-1/e) approximation for the cardinality‑constrained problem, directly justifying the Belief‑Conditioned One‑step Diffusion (B‑COD) principle: activate sensors greedily until the predicted belief uncertainty drops below a safety threshold.

