7. Safety Integration via Hierarchical Barrier Functions

Safety is enforced through three continuously differentiable control barrier functions (CBFs):

· Physical: h_{\text{phys}}(x)\ge 0 (joint limits, self‑collision, center of mass within support polygon).
· Force: h_{\text{force}}(x,c)\ge 0 (friction cone, contact force limits; may require high‑order CBFs).
· Belief: h_{\text{belief}}(b)\ge 0 (trace of the estimation error covariance below a maximum \sigma_{\max}^2).

A contact c is safe from belief b if the combined CBF‑CLF quadratic program (QP) is feasible. To avoid mathematically problematic infinite values, we impose a large but finite penalty:

\operatorname{FCA}(b,c) = \operatorname{FCA}_{\text{safe}}(b,c) \;-\; M \cdot \mathbb{I}[\text{QP infeasible at } b],

with M>0. Exact penalty theory guarantees the existence of a finite threshold M^* such that for all M\ge M^*, the optimal policy of the penalized problem coincides with the hard‑constrained optimal policy (Theorem 5). This formulation avoids infinite values while preserving safety guarantees.

