8. Approximate Planning and Performance Guarantee

In practice, FCA is computed with (i) an approximate value network \widetilde V yielding \widetilde{\operatorname{FCA}} with error \varepsilon_{\text{FCA}} = \max_{b,c}|\operatorname{FCA}(b,c)-\widetilde{\operatorname{FCA}}(b,c)|, (ii) a complementarity‑free contact solver introducing a solver optimality gap \varepsilon_{\text{solver}}, and (iii) planning limited to H macro‑steps of maximum primitive length L_{\max}.

Theorem 6 (Integrated Suboptimality Bound).
The greedy policy \tilde\pi with respect to \widetilde{\operatorname{FCA}} satisfies

V^{\tilde\pi}(b) \;\ge\; V^*(b) \;-\; \Delta_{\text{horizon}} \;-\; \Delta_{\text{approx}} \;-\; \Delta_{\text{solver}},

where

\begin{aligned}
\Delta_{\text{horizon}} &= \frac{2\gamma^{H L_{\max}+1} R_{\max}}{(1-\gamma)^2}, \\[2mm]
\Delta_{\text{approx}} &= \frac{2\varepsilon_{\text{FCA}}}{(1-\gamma)^2}, \\[2mm]
\Delta_{\text{solver}} &= \frac{\varepsilon_{\text{solver}}}{1-\gamma}.
\end{aligned}

Proof sketch. The horizon truncation introduces an error in the value function of at most \varepsilon_{\text{horizon}} = \gamma^{H L_{\max}} R_{\max}/(1-\gamma). Using an approximate value function with total supremum‑norm error \varepsilon = \varepsilon_{\text{horizon}} + \varepsilon_{\text{FCA}}, the performance loss of the greedy policy relative to the optimal is bounded by 2\gamma\varepsilon/(1-\gamma) (standard approximate dynamic programming result). Substituting \varepsilon yields the first two terms. The solver gap adds an independent per‑step action‑value error of at most \varepsilon_{\text{solver}}, which accumulates to \varepsilon_{\text{solver}}/(1-\gamma) over the infinite horizon. The three error sources act on orthogonal aspects of the decision process and sum by the triangle inequality. ∎

Macro‑Action Planning. Offline, a library of macro‑actions (open‑loop contact sequences) is generated using exact LCP dynamics. Online, the robot uses complementarity‑free CI‑MPC that closes the loop within each macro‑step, with a total variation error \delta_{\text{cf}} relative to the true dynamics. If the true transition kernel is P^* and the approximate kernel is \widehat P, the resulting value functions satisfy

|V^{\text{offline}} - V^{\text{online}}| \;\le\; \frac{\gamma L_V}{1-\gamma}\, \delta_{\text{cf}},

assuming V^* is L_V-Lipschitz with respect to state distributions in total variation (Theorem 8). This gap is absorbed into the solver error \varepsilon_{\text{solver}} when the online planner refines the macro‑action.

