9. Computational Architecture

The FCA framework is realized through a three‑timescale architecture:

· Offline (hours): The IMPACT solver precomputes a library of macro‑actions using exact linear complementarity dynamics. Amortized cost: O(|\mathcal{M}| \cdot T_{\text{IMPACT}}).
· Online (10–100 Hz): For the current belief, a complementarity‑free reduced‑order CI‑MPC evaluates candidate contacts from the safe set, with lookahead H. Each evaluation costs O(T_{\text{MPC}}). The per‑decision complexity is O(|\mathcal{C}_{\text{safe}}|\cdot H \cdot T_{\text{MPC}}).
· Reactive (1 kHz): A CBF‑CLF quadratic program enforces safety constraints in real time, with constant time complexity.

Sensor selection adds O(|\text{Sensors}|\log|\text{Sensors}|) using the greedy algorithm. The architecture respects the bounded‑computation axiom and is feasible on current humanoid hardware.

10. Validation Roadmap and Experimental Plan

The Future Contact Advantage framework is a mathematical construction: its value lies as much in the guarantees it provides as in its implementability on physical hardware. This chapter outlines a comprehensive roadmap for validating the theory through a progression of simulation studies and hardware experiments on a full‑sized humanoid robot. The plan is designed to test each theoretical claim individually and then to demonstrate the integrated system performing contact‑rich tasks under real‑world uncertainty.

10.1 Experimental Platform

All hardware experiments will be conducted on Atlas‑X, a 1.8 m, 85 kg humanoid robot with 28 torque‑controlled degrees of freedom, six‑axis force‑torque sensors at the ankles and wrists, an inertial measurement unit (IMU) in the pelvis, joint encoders, and a multi‑camera perception system providing point‑cloud and RGB‑D data. Two additional tactile sensor arrays (uSkin, 36 taxels each) are mounted on the palms and finger‑tips. The onboard computation consists of a quad‑core Intel i7 CPU for real‑time control and an NVIDIA Jetson AGX Orin for neural network inference and optimization. The robot communicates with an external workstation via a dedicated Ethernet link for offline library building and logging.

10.2 Staged Evaluation

The validation proceeds through five stages, each isolating a different component of the FCA framework before combining them into full system demonstrations.

Stage 1: Geometric Filtering in Simulation

Objective: Verify Theorem 1—that contacts with large locomotion projections are indeed more valuable for task progress.

Setup: We simulate the robot in a simplified environment: a flat ground plane, a vertical ladder with rungs at 0.3 m spacing, and a peg‑in‑hole insertion station. For each of 500 randomly sampled configurations on \mathcal{Q}_{\text{adm}}, we instantiate 200 contact candidates (hand grasps on rungs at various heights, foot placements, etc.). For each candidate, we compute the contact map \mathbf{A}_c using forward kinematics and finite differences, perform the Hodge–Helmholtz decomposition, and project the locomotion component \mathbf{v}_{\text{loc}} onto task directions (upward for climbing, forward for locomotion, insertion axis for peg‑in‑hole). We then run a short open‑loop trajectory (0.5 s) under each contact and measure the true task reward (distance moved, insertion depth).

Expected Outcome: The Spearman rank correlation between the geometric score \max\langle\mathbf{v}_{\text{loc}},\mathbf{d}\rangle and the achieved task reward should exceed 0.8, confirming that geometry alone can serve as a strong filter. Contacts whose locomotion projection is below a threshold can be discarded with minimal loss in performance, reducing the online candidate set by 60–80%.

Stage 2: Safety Filter Validation

Objective: Demonstrate that the hierarchical CBF‑CLF‑QP reliably enforces safety constraints without impeding task execution.

Setup: We program the robot to perform a ladder‑climbing motion with pre‑computed contact sequences, and subject it to random external pushes (0–150 N applied at the torso) and noisy torque measurements (Gaussian, 1 Nm standard deviation). The reactive QP runs at 1 kHz with three barrier functions: joint limits (\pm 2^\circ from hardware limits), center‑of‑mass within a support polygon expanded by 2 cm, and contact force within 80% of the friction cone limit. The soft penalty constant M is initially set to 10^6 and reduced until the first safety violation is observed, then doubled for the final experiments.

Expected Outcome: The robot should complete the ladder climb in over 95% of 200 trials without a single joint‑limit violation, fall, or slip. The addition of the belief barrier (limiting estimation error covariance) should become necessary only under aggressive perturbations, at which point it should cause the QP to become infeasible before a dangerous state is reached, triggering a contact re‑plan.

Stage 3: Information Gain and Sensor Selection

Objective: Verify the submodular sensor selection (Theorem 4) and the value‑of‑information bound (Theorem 3).

Setup: In a peg‑in‑hole task with uncertain hole position (known to within \pm 2 cm), the robot must choose which sensors to activate during approach. We compare four policies: (1) activate all sensors always, (2) random subset, (3) greedy selection maximizing mutual information per unit cost, and (4) the B‑COD policy that stops when predicted covariance drops below a threshold. The information gain is computed offline by sampling the belief distribution using a particle filter, and the mutual information is estimated via Monte Carlo integration of the KL divergence. We measure the task completion time, sensing energy cost, and final insertion accuracy.

Expected Outcome: The greedy and B‑COD policies should achieve a mutual information that is at least (1-1/e) of the full‑sensor policy (the submodular bound). The actual value gain (task completion time) should correlate positively with the square root of mutual information, consistent with Theorem 3, with the constant L/\sqrt{2} estimated from the simulation data.

Stage 4: Integrated Offline‑Online‑Reactive Architecture

Objective: Demonstrate that the three‑timescale architecture achieves near‑optimal behavior within the theoretical suboptimality bounds of Theorem 6.

Setup: We generate a macro‑action library containing 10 000 sequences for ladder climbing (5 rungs) and peg insertion using the IMPACT solver with exact LCP dynamics (offline, ~8 h computation). Online, the robot runs CI‑MPC at 50 Hz with a lookahead of H=3 macro‑steps, evaluating only the contacts that pass the geometric and safety filters (\mathcal{C}_{\text{safe}} typically 5–15 candidates). The value network \widetilde V_\psi is a three‑layer MLP trained on 10⁶ rollouts from the macro‑action library, with a gradient penalty ensuring a Lipschitz constant L\approx 2.3. The Wasserstein radius \epsilon is calibrated as the 95th percentile of empirical belief prediction errors observed during training rollouts (\epsilon = 0.07 in belief‑space W_1 distance).

We compare the FCA greedy policy against three baselines:

· Hand‑crafted: A finite‑state machine (FSM) with contact transitions designed by an expert.
· PPO‑end‑to‑end: Proximal Policy Optimization trained directly in simulation for 2\times 10^7 timesteps, with the same reward function but no explicit contact structure.
· Oracle: The optimal policy computed via value iteration on a discretized belief space (possible only in a simplified 2D environment).

For each policy, we measure the average discounted return over 100 episodes with random initial configurations and external disturbances.

Expected Outcome: The FCA policy should achieve returns within 10% of the oracle (which is computed with infinite horizon and exact dynamics). The gap should be decomposable into the three terms of Theorem 6. By toggling the value network accuracy and horizon length, we will verify that the empirical \Delta_{\text{approx}} and \Delta_{\text{horizon}} scale as predicted.

Stage 5: Full‑System Hardware Demonstration

Objective: Show that the FCA framework enables the humanoid robot to perform a complex, multi‑phase task—ascending a ladder, crossing a narrow platform, and inserting a plug into a socket—under real‑world sensing noise and model mismatch.

Setup: The task is continuous: climb a 1.5 m ladder, walk across a 0.3 m‑wide beam, and insert a USB‑style plug into a receptacle with 1 mm clearance. The robot’s world model includes an initial rough CAD map; the true environment has slight deviations (0.5–1 cm offsets in rung positions, a slightly tilted beam). No manual recalibration is allowed. The robot must autonomously select contacts, plan trajectories, and execute the task end‑to‑end. The FCA policy is used throughout. We will report: task success rate, average completion time, total energy consumption, and the number of safety‑related replans.

Expected Outcome: The FCA‑guided robot should achieve a success rate above 80% over 30 trials, compared to an expected rate below 30% for the hand‑crafted FSM (which is brittle to model errors) and near zero for the purely learned PPO policy (which suffers from sim‑to‑real transfer). The robot will actively use sensor information to reduce uncertainty during the plug insertion phase, validating the B‑COD principle on hardware. We will record the per‑decision FCA values and confirm that the chosen contacts consistently achieve near‑zero FCA (optimal), while unsafe or non‑informative contacts are correctly penalized.

10.3 Data Collection and Metrics

All experiments will be logged at 1 kHz: joint states, commanded torques, belief estimates, FCA scores for all evaluated contacts, QP feasibility flags, and task‑specific rewards. The primary performance metrics are:

· Task success rate (binary).
· Discounted total reward \sum_t \gamma^t r_t.
· Safety violations (number of times a CBF constraint is violated).
· Information gain per contact (measured mutual information, compared to the greedy bound).
· Computation time per decision cycle (online MPC + reactive QP).

The theoretical bounds in Theorem 6 will be compared against the empirical suboptimality gap \widetilde V^{\tilde\pi} - V^* estimated from the oracle policy in the simplified simulation.

