11. Conclusion and Future Work

The Future Contact Advantage (FCA) framework establishes a normative, resource‑aware calculus for contact‑rich robot motion. By defining contact selection as the maximization of a single scalar advantage function, the approach fuses geometric reasoning, distributional robustness, information gain, and safety constraints into a unified decision that can be computed in real time on humanoid hardware. We have proved that this greedy policy is near‑optimal under four sources of error—horizon truncation, value function approximation, solver suboptimality, and model ambiguity—each explicitly bounded. The geometric lower bound (Theorem 1) provides a purely kinematic filter that operates independently of learned components; the Wasserstein robustness bound (Theorem 2) gives a principled way to hedge against model mismatch; the information‑theoretic bound (Theorem 3) links sensory actions to provable improvements in expected value; and the submodular sensor selection (Theorem 4) guarantees near‑optimal information gathering at low computational cost.

The principal contribution is a normative standard: any contact decision‑maker, be it hand‑crafted, learned, or hybrid, can be evaluated by its achieved Future Contact Advantage, with the theoretical bounds serving as a benchmark. This bridges the gap between the formal guarantees of model‑based robotics and the scalability of learning‑based methods.

The experimental roadmap outlined in Chapter 10 will subject these claims to rigorous physical testing. If successful, the FCA framework will demonstrate that a humanoid robot can climb a ladder, traverse a beam, and perform a precise insertion with minimal human intervention, all while respecting safety and computational budgets. The empirical decomposition of the suboptimality gap will either confirm the tightness of our bounds or reveal where the analysis can be sharpened.

Future work. Several extensions are natural. First, the Lipschitz constants of the value function, which appear in the robustness and information bounds, are currently estimated conservatively; learning a local Lipschitz certificate could tighten these guarantees by orders of magnitude, making them practically meaningful rather than vacuous. Second, the framework assumes rigid‑body contacts; extending the Hodge–Helmholtz decomposition to soft and compliant contact modes would broaden applicability to legged locomotion on deformable terrain and manipulation of flexible objects. Third, the macro‑action library generation currently enumerates all sequences of short length; incorporating learned priors or using reachability‑guided search could scale the offline phase to vastly larger libraries. Fourth, the exact penalty constant M for safety is tuned empirically; an adaptive scheme that adjusts M online based on the predicted probability of safety violation could improve performance without compromising guarantees. Finally, while the FCA is a greedy policy with bounded suboptimality, a full tree‑search or Monte Carlo planning variant could close the remaining optimality gap in exchange for higher computation, offering a tunable spectrum from reactive to deliberative contact intelligence.

In summary, the FCA theory provides a rigorous mathematical language for reasoning about contact decisions in physically interactive robots. The planned experiments will determine whether this language translates into robust, capable behavior in the real world. If it does, the framework can serve as a foundation for the next generation of humanoid machines that move confidently and safely through complex, uncertain environments.

