Appendix A: Proof of Theorem 1 (Geometric Lower Bound)

Theorem 1. Let V_c = \operatorname{Im}(\mathbf{A}_c) be the subspace of body velocities achievable under contact c, and let \mathcal{V}_{\text{adm}} be the set of body velocities admissible under torque and stability constraints on the compact admissible configuration manifold \mathcal{Q}_{\text{adm}}. Assume the task reward R_{\text{task}}(b,a) is strictly increasing in the projection \langle \mathbf{v}, \mathbf{d}\rangle of the expected body velocity onto a fixed direction \mathbf{d}\in\mathfrak{se}(3). Then

\max_{a\in\mathcal{A}_c(b)} R_{\text{task}}(b,a) \;\ge\; R_{\text{task,max}}\!\left( \max_{\mathbf{v}\in V_c \cap \mathcal{V}_{\text{adm}}} \langle \mathbf{v}_{\text{loc}}, \mathbf{d}\rangle \right),

where \mathbf{v}_{\text{loc}} is the locomotion component from the Hodge–Helmholtz decomposition.

Proof.
The robot’s dynamics on \mathcal{Q}_{\text{adm}} are fully actuated in the joint space, with control inputs \tau (torques). The system is of the form

\dot x = f(x) + \sum_{i} g_i(x) \tau_i,

where x = (q,\dot q) and the vector fields are smooth. On the compact set \mathcal{Q}_{\text{adm}} the velocity space is a linear space, and the mapping from joint torques to joint accelerations is given by the inverse inertia matrix. Although torques directly influence accelerations, small‑time local controllability (STLC) on the constrained configuration manifold guarantees that any velocity in the convex hull of \mathcal{V}_{\text{adm}} can be approached arbitrarily closely over a short time horizon using bounded controls. Specifically, for any \mathbf{v} \in V_c \cap \mathcal{V}_{\text{adm}} and any \delta > 0, there exists a control policy a_{[0,\Delta t]} such that the resulting average body velocity over [0,\Delta t] is within \delta of \mathbf{v} and the terminal configuration remains in \mathcal{Q}_{\text{adm}}.

Because the reward R_{\text{task}} depends monotonically on the expected body velocity, we may take a sequence of actions approximating any feasible velocity. Consequently,

\max_{a} R_{\text{task}}(b,a) \;\ge\; \sup_{\mathbf{v} \in V_c \cap \mathcal{V}_{\text{adm}}} R_{\text{task}}(\mathbf{v}) \;=\; R_{\text{task,max}}\!\left( \max_{\mathbf{v} \in V_c \cap \mathcal{V}_{\text{adm}}} \langle \mathbf{v}, \mathbf{d}\rangle \right).

The Hodge–Helmholtz decomposition on the compact manifold (see Lemma H.2) is orthogonal in the kinetic energy metric, so \langle \mathbf{v}, \mathbf{d}\rangle = \langle \mathbf{v}_{\text{loc}}, \mathbf{d}\rangle + \langle \mathbf{v}_{\text{curl}}, \mathbf{d}\rangle. Restricting the maximisation to the subspace \mathbf{v}_{\text{curl}} = 0 yields the lower bound in terms of the pure locomotion component:

\max_{\mathbf{v} \in V_c \cap \mathcal{V}_{\text{adm}}} \langle \mathbf{v}, \mathbf{d}\rangle \;\ge\; \max_{\mathbf{v} \in V_c \cap \mathcal{V}_{\text{adm}}} \langle \mathbf{v}_{\text{loc}}, \mathbf{d}\rangle .

Combining the two inequalities completes the proof. ∎

