Appendix B: Proof of Theorem 2 (Wasserstein Robustness)

Theorem 2. Let \hat p(\cdot \mid b,c,a) be the nominal belief transition, and define the Wasserstein ambiguity set

\mathfrak{P}(b,c,a) = \{\, p \mid W_1(p, \hat p) \le \epsilon \,\},

with W_1 the 1‑Wasserstein distance on the belief space \mathcal{B} metrized by a suitable distance \rho. Assume the optimal value function V^* : \mathcal{B} \to \mathbb{R} is L-Lipschitz with respect to W_1, i.e., |V^*(b_1) - V^*(b_2)| \le L\,W_1(b_1, b_2). Then

\operatorname{FCA}_{\text{rob}}(b,c) \;\ge\; \operatorname{FCA}_{\text{nom}}(b,c) \;-\; \gamma L \epsilon .

Proof.
For any fixed action a, the Kantorovich–Rubinstein duality (Lemma H.3) states

W_1(p,q) = \sup_{\phi : \|\phi\|_{\text{Lip}}\le 1} \bigl( \mathbb{E}_p[\phi] - \mathbb{E}_q[\phi] \bigr).

Because V^* is L-Lipschitz, \phi = V^*/L is 1‑Lipschitz. Therefore, for any p with W_1(p,\hat p)\le \epsilon,

\mathbb{E}_p[V^*] \;\ge\; \mathbb{E}_{\hat p}[V^*] - L\,W_1(p,\hat p) \;\ge\; \mathbb{E}_{\hat p}[V^*] - L\epsilon .

Taking the infimum over p\in\mathfrak{P} preserves the inequality. The robust FCA is then

\begin{aligned}
\operatorname{FCA}_{\text{rob}}(b,c) 
&= \max_{a} \Bigl( r(b,c,a) + \gamma \inf_{p\in\mathfrak{P}} \mathbb{E}_{p}[V^*] \Bigr) - V^*(b) \\
&\ge \max_{a} \bigl( r + \gamma \mathbb{E}_{\hat p}[V^*] - \gamma L \epsilon \bigr) - V^*(b) \\
&= \bigl( \max_{a}(r + \gamma \mathbb{E}_{\hat p}[V^*]) - V^*(b) \bigr) - \gamma L \epsilon \\
&= \operatorname{FCA}_{\text{nom}}(b,c) - \gamma L \epsilon .
\end{aligned}

Thus the robust FCA is at most \gamma L\epsilon worse than the nominal FCA. ∎

