Appendix C: Proof of Theorem 3 (Value of Information Lower Bound)

Theorem 3. Assume the optimal value function V^* is L-Lipschitz with respect to the total variation distance on beliefs: |V^*(b) - V^*(b')| \le L\,\|b - b'\|_{\mathrm{TV}}. Then for any contact c,

\mathbb{E}\bigl[V^*(b'_c)\bigr] - \mathbb{E}\bigl[V^*(b'_0)\bigr] \;\ge\; \frac{L}{\sqrt{2}} \sqrt{\,I(X'; Z \mid b,c)},

where b'_c is the posterior belief after an observation under contact c, and b'_0 is the prior belief (no observation).

Proof.
Let p(z) be the marginal distribution of the observation Z under contact c, and let b^z denote the posterior belief after observing z. The mutual information between the state X' and Z is

I(X';Z \mid b,c) = \mathbb{E}_{z \sim p}\bigl[ D_{\mathrm{KL}}(b^z \| b) \bigr],

where b = b'_0 is the prior belief (the belief without the observation). Pinsker’s inequality (Lemma H.4) bounds the total variation distance by the KL divergence:

\|b^z - b\|_{\mathrm{TV}} \le \sqrt{\frac12 D_{\mathrm{KL}}(b^z \| b)} .

Because V^* is L-Lipschitz in total variation,

V^*(b^z) - V^*(b) \;\ge\; -L\,\|b^z - b\|_{\mathrm{TV}} \;\ge\; -L \sqrt{\frac12 D_{\mathrm{KL}}(b^z \| b)} .

Since this holds for every z, we take expectations over z:

\begin{aligned}
\mathbb{E}\bigl[V^*(b'_c)\bigr] - V^*(b) 
&= \mathbb{E}_z\bigl[ V^*(b^z) - V^*(b) \bigr] \\
&\ge -L\,\mathbb{E}_z\!\left[ \sqrt{\frac12 D_{\mathrm{KL}}(b^z \| b)} \right] \\
&\ge -L \sqrt{\frac12 \mathbb{E}_z\bigl[ D_{\mathrm{KL}}(b^z \| b) \bigr]} \quad \text{(Jensen, since }\sqrt{\cdot}\text{ is concave)} \\
&= - \frac{L}{\sqrt{2}} \sqrt{ I(X';Z \mid b,c) } .
\end{aligned}

The left‑hand side is the expected value difference \mathbb{E}[V^*(b'_c)] - \mathbb{E}[V^*(b'_0)] (since b'_0 = b). Reversing the inequality sign yields the stated lower bound:

\mathbb{E}[V^*(b'_c)] - \mathbb{E}[V^*(b'_0)] \;\ge\; \frac{L}{\sqrt{2}} \sqrt{ I(X';Z \mid b,c) } .

This bound is universal and avoids degenerate constants. ∎

