Appendix D: Proof of Theorem 4 (Submodular Sensor Selection)

Theorem 4. For fixed belief b and contact c, the set function f(\mathcal{Z}) = I(X'; Z_\mathcal{Z} \mid b,c) defined on sensor subsets \mathcal{Z} \subseteq \mathcal{S} is monotone and submodular.

Proof.
Let \mathcal{S} = \text{Sensors}(c). Monotonicity is obvious: adding sensors cannot decrease mutual information. For submodularity, take any \mathcal{A} \subseteq \mathcal{B} \subseteq \mathcal{S} and a new sensor s \notin \mathcal{B}. Then

\begin{aligned}
f(\mathcal{A}\cup\{s\}) - f(\mathcal{A}) 
&= I(X'; Z_{\mathcal{A}\cup\{s\}}) - I(X'; Z_\mathcal{A}) \\
&= I(X'; Z_s \mid Z_\mathcal{A}) \qquad \text{(chain rule)}.
\end{aligned}

Similarly, f(\mathcal{B}\cup\{s\}) - f(\mathcal{B}) = I(X'; Z_s \mid Z_\mathcal{B}). Because conditioning reduces entropy (the data‑processing inequality for mutual information), we have I(X'; Z_s \mid Z_\mathcal{A}) \ge I(X'; Z_s \mid Z_\mathcal{B}). Hence

f(\mathcal{A}\cup\{s\}) - f(\mathcal{A}) \ge f(\mathcal{B}\cup\{s\}) - f(\mathcal{B}),

which is exactly submodularity. ∎

The standard result for monotone submodular functions guarantees that the greedy algorithm (iteratively add the sensor with the highest marginal gain) achieves at least (1-1/e) of the optimal value for cardinality‑constrained maximization, and a similar bound for budgeted maximization.

