Appendix E: Proof of Theorem 5 (Safety‑Conditioned FCA with Soft Penalty)

Theorem 5. Let h_{\text{phys}}, h_{\text{force}}, h_{\text{belief}} be valid control barrier functions (CBFs) defining a safe set \mathcal{S}. For a contact c and belief b, define

\operatorname{FCA}(b,c) = \operatorname{FCA}_{\text{safe}}(b,c) \;-\; M \cdot \mathbb{I}[\,\text{CBF‑CLF‑QP infeasible at } b\,],

where \operatorname{FCA}_{\text{safe}} is the optimal value of the constrained POMDP with state‑action safety constraints. Then there exists a finite M^* such that for all M \ge M^*, the maximizer of the penalized FCA coincides with the optimal safe contact, and \operatorname{FCA} is finite everywhere.

Proof.
The unconstrained POMDP with the reward augmented by -M whenever the QP is infeasible is a standard exact penalty formulation. Because the state and action spaces are continuous and the CBFs are smooth, the optimal value of the penalized MDP converges uniformly to the value of the constrained MDP as M\to\infty. Since the value function of the constrained problem is bounded, there exists a finite threshold M^* beyond which the penalty is larger than any possible advantage gained by leaving the safe set; thus the optimal policy of the penalized problem respects the constraints exactly. Consequently, for M \ge M^*, \operatorname{FCA}_{\text{safe}} equals the constrained optimum and the indicator term correctly flags infeasible contacts without introducing infinite values. ∎

