Appendix F: Proof of Theorem 6 (Integrated Suboptimality Bound)

Theorem 6. Suppose we compute an approximate advantage \widetilde{\operatorname{FCA}} with error \varepsilon_{\text{FCA}} = \max_{b,c}|\operatorname{FCA}(b,c)-\widetilde{\operatorname{FCA}}(b,c)|, use a trajectory optimizer with solver gap \varepsilon_{\text{solver}}, and plan with a finite horizon of H macro‑steps each of maximum length L_{\max}. Let \tilde\pi be the greedy policy that selects the contact and action maximizing \widetilde{\operatorname{FCA}} online. Then

V^{\tilde\pi}(b) \;\ge\; V^*(b) \;-\; \Delta_{\text{horizon}} \;-\; \Delta_{\text{approx}} \;-\; \Delta_{\text{solver}},

with

\Delta_{\text{horizon}} = \frac{2\gamma^{H L_{\max}+1} R_{\max}}{(1-\gamma)^2},\quad
\Delta_{\text{approx}} = \frac{2\varepsilon_{\text{FCA}}}{(1-\gamma)^2},\quad
\Delta_{\text{solver}} = \frac{\varepsilon_{\text{solver}}}{1-\gamma}.

Proof.
We decompose the suboptimality into three independent components.

Horizon truncation. Let V_H be the value function of the policy that, at each step, solves the exact H L_{\max}-step receding‑horizon problem (using the true model and infinite‑horizon value function at the tail). Standard approximate dynamic programming bounds give

\|V_H - V^*\|_\infty \le \frac{\gamma^{H L_{\max}} R_{\max}}{1-\gamma}.

The greedy policy using the H-step lookahead thus suffers an additional performance loss of at most 2\gamma \varepsilon_{\text{horizon}}/(1-\gamma) relative to V^*, where \varepsilon_{\text{horizon}} = \gamma^{H L_{\max}} R_{\max}/(1-\gamma). This yields

\Delta_{\text{horizon}} = \frac{2\gamma^{H L_{\max}+1} R_{\max}}{(1-\gamma)^2}.

Advantage approximation. The policy \tilde\pi greedily maximizes \widetilde{\operatorname{FCA}} instead of the true FCA. In an MDP, the performance difference between the optimal policy and a policy that makes an error bounded by \varepsilon_{\text{FCA}} in the advantage function at each state is at most \frac{2\varepsilon_{\text{FCA}}}{(1-\gamma)^2}. This is a standard result from approximate policy iteration and applies to our contact‑level advantage because FCA is itself an advantage. Thus

\Delta_{\text{approx}} = \frac{2\varepsilon_{\text{FCA}}}{(1-\gamma)^2}.

Solver suboptimality. The online trajectory optimizer returns actions that are within \varepsilon_{\text{solver}} of the true optimal Q-value for a given contact. This induces a one‑step value gap of at most \varepsilon_{\text{solver}}. Over the infinite discounted horizon, the cumulative loss is \varepsilon_{\text{solver}}/(1-\gamma).

The three error sources operate on different aspects of the decision process (planning depth, value function accuracy, and action optimization) and are additive by the triangle inequality. Summing them gives the stated guarantee. ∎

