Appendix H: Supporting Lemmas

H.1 Lipschitz Continuity of the Optimal Value Function

Lemma H.1. Under a bounded reward |r|\le R_{\max} and a discount factor \gamma\in(0,1), if the belief transition kernel p(b'|b,a) is K-Lipschitz in the sense that \|p(\cdot|b_1,a) - p(\cdot|b_2,a)\|_{\mathrm{TV}} \le K \, d(b_1,b_2) for a metric d on \mathcal{B}, then the optimal value function V^* is L-Lipschitz with

L = \frac{R_{\max} K}{(1-\gamma)^2}.

Proof sketch. The Bellman operator \mathcal{T} is a \gamma-contraction in the sup norm and, under the kernel Lipschitz condition, also preserves a Lipschitz constant bound. Iterating from any bounded function yields a fixed point with Lipschitz constant as above. The (1-\gamma)^{-2} factor arises from the geometric series in the bound propagation.

H.2 Hodge Decomposition on SO(3)

Lemma H.2. On the compact Lie group SO(3) with bi‑invariant Riemannian metric, any smooth 1‑form \omega can be uniquely decomposed as \omega = d f + \delta \beta, where f is a smooth function and \beta is a smooth 2‑form. This yields the exact–co‑exact splitting used in the text.

H.3 Kantorovich–Rubinstein Duality

Lemma H.3. For any Polish space (X,\rho) and probability measures p,q,

W_1(p,q) = \sup_{\phi : \|\phi\|_{\text{Lip}}\le 1} \bigl( \mathbb{E}_p[\phi] - \mathbb{E}_q[\phi] \bigr).

H.4 Pinsker’s Inequality

Lemma H.4. For any two probability measures p,q on the same measurable space,

\|p - q\|_{\mathrm{TV}} \le \sqrt{\frac12 D_{\mathrm{KL}}(p \| q)}.

H.5 Small‑Time Local Controllability on \mathcal{Q}_{\text{adm}}

Lemma H.5. The fully‑actuated robot dynamics are small‑time locally controllable on the compact admissible configuration manifold \mathcal{Q}_{\text{adm}}, meaning that for any q_0\in\mathcal{Q}_{\text{adm}} and any neighborhood, the reachable set for arbitrarily small times contains an open set. Consequently, any feasible velocity in the convex hull of \mathcal{V}_{\text{adm}} can be approximated arbitrarily well.

Proof. The system is driftless after feedback linearization and satisfies the Lie algebra rank condition; the result follows from standard STLC theorems (e.g., Sussmann’s condition). The compactness of \mathcal{Q}_{\text{adm}} ensures uniform bounds on the control efforts. ∎


